{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>You shall not curse Elohim.</b> He warns concerning the judge, that one must not curse him when he has found him liable in judgment. And this law is juxtaposed to 'If you lend money' because sometimes a dispute arises between lender and borrower, and they must come before the judge who will judge between them, and perhaps the one found liable in judgment will curse him — therefore he warns concerning him." "<b>And a leader among your people.</b> This is the king, who is the leader over the people, and Scripture warns that if the king finds him liable in his judgment, he must not curse him. Thus Solomon, peace be upon him, warned (Ecclesiastes 10:20): ״גם במדעך מלך אל תקלל ובחדרי משכבך אל תקלל עשיר כי עוף השמים יוליך את הקול״ — 'Even in your thought do not curse the king, and in the chambers of your bed do not curse the rich, for a bird of the heavens will carry the voice.' And he placed the judge, who is a sage, before the king; and so they said, of blessed memory, in the order of ranks: a sage precedes a king. And the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded that this verse is a warning against cursing HaShem, even by an epithet, and He first warned concerning the supreme King, blessed be He, and afterward concerning the king who reigns on earth. And so Solomon said (Proverbs 24:21): ״ירא את ה' בני ומלך״ — 'Fear HaShem, my son, and the king.'" "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> ״אלהים לא תקלל״ — and it is written ״מלאתך ודמעך״. If you curse the judges, you curse your own produce. You find that when the judges are cursed, the grain diminishes, as it is said (Ruth 1:1): ״ויהי בימי שפט השפטים ויהי רעב״ — 'And it came to pass in the days when the judges judged, and there was a famine'; therefore it says ״מלאתך ודמעך״ and so on. Do not separate them out of order, but in order: terumah first, then first tithe, then second tithe. And if you separated them in order, you will have sons who are males, as it is said: ״בכור בניך תתן לי״ — 'The firstborn of your sons you shall give to Me.' ״כן תעשה לשורך״ — so shall you do for your ox: if you gave, you are not giving from your own, but from Mine; and so it says (I Chronicles 29:14): ״כי ממך הכל ומידך נתנו לך״ — 'For all is from You, and from Your hand we have given to You.' And if you do thus, ״ואנשי קדש תהיון לי״ — 'you shall be men of holiness to Me' — the Holy One, blessed be He, will make you His terumah of the world, as it is said (Jeremiah 2:3): ״קדש ישראל לה' ראשית תבואתו״ — 'Israel is holiness to HaShem, the first of His produce.' And because you are terumah, you have no permission to eat terefah, as it is said: ״ובשר בשדה טרפה״. The Holy One, blessed be He, said: you are obligated to the dogs. At the hour when I killed the firstborn of Egypt, and the Egyptians were burying their dead at night, the dogs barked at them, but to Israel they said nothing, as it is said (Exodus 11:7): ״ולכל בני ישראל לא יחרץ כלב לשונו״ — 'Against any of the children of Israel, no dog shall sharpen its tongue'; therefore ״לכלב תשליכון אותו״ — 'to the dog you shall cast it.' And concerning this it is said: ״ואנשי קדש תהיון לי״. Thus far in the Midrash."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:03:17.321982245Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}